{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And I shall return in peace to my father's house.</b> It is possible to say that everything that Jacob uttered from his mouth in this vow was all fulfilled in him. 'And will keep me on this journey' — indeed the Holy One, blessed be He, kept him on that journey. 'And the bread' is the women he took, in the language of (Exodus 2:20) ״קראן לו ויאכל לחם״, and likewise (Genesis 39:6) ״כי אם את הלחם אשר הוא אוכל״. 'And the clothing' is that he grew wealthy through Laban's flocks, for garments are made of wool. <b>And I shall return in peace</b> — this is what is written, ״ויבא יעקב שלם״. 'And HaShem will be to me for Elohim' — this is what is written, ״ויקרא לו אל אלהי ישראל״." "<b>And HaShem will be to me for Elohim.</b> This is not a condition, heaven forbid, but rather a vow. He says that if he returns to the land of Israel, where his father Isaac's house is, the special Name will be to him for Elohim, for there he will worship Him in the special land. And this is by way of the saying of our Rabbis of blessed memory: whoever dwells outside the land is like one who has no Elohim."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:59.172092524Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}